Let an app's package.json "imports" override the framework-seam conventions.

TanStack Start's runtime imports five bare specifiers a bundler is expected to
fill in, `#tanstack-router-entry` chief among them: it becomes the app's
`getRouter`. The framework makes that path configurable -- the Vite plugin
resolves `router.entry` (and `srcDirectory`) through `resolveEntry` and aliases
the specifier at the resolved file. oj hardcodes `<app>/src/router`, the
framework default, in three places: the SSR loader's ALIASES table, and the
`routerEntry()` that feeds rolldown's `resolve.alias` in each of the dev client
bundle and the production build.

An app that sets `router: { entry: "lib/router" }` therefore builds under Vite
and fails under oj:

    [RESOLVE_ERROR] Could not resolve '#tanstack-router-entry' in
      .../@tanstack/start-client-core/dist/esm/client/hydrateStart.js
      Matched alias not found for '#tanstack-router-entry'

and there is nothing the app can do about it. The loader already implements
package.json `imports`, and declaring one is the standard way to name a
subpath -- but the conventions are consulted first, so a declaration loses to a
default.

This inverts that order for `#`-prefixed specifiers everywhere the seam is
resolved. An app with no `imports` entry resolves exactly as before, so the
framework defaults keep working; an app that has said where its entry is now
gets what it said.

The fuller fix is for the adapter to read the app's Start configuration, which
would also settle `srcDirectory` and the client/server/start entries; that
needs the Vite config to reach `start_dev`, which today receives none. This is
the smaller half: it gives an app a way to say what oj cannot yet infer.

Carried here so //oj:dev_server can serve an app that configures its router
entry. Reported upstream; no open PR covers it as of oj 0.1.8.

diff --git a/src/assets/start/build.mjs b/src/assets/start/build.mjs
--- a/src/assets/start/build.mjs
+++ b/src/assets/start/build.mjs
@@ -63,7 +63,24 @@
   '',
 ].join("\n");
 
+// The app's package.json "imports" wins over the src/router convention, the
+// way it does in the SSR loader: the framework makes this path configurable
+// (`router.entry`), and an app that moved it has to be able to say so.
+function declaredRouterEntry() {
+  try {
+    const target = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(APP, "package.json"), "utf8"))
+      .imports?.["#tanstack-router-entry"];
+    if (typeof target !== "string") return null;
+    const p = resolve(APP, target);
+    return existsSync(p) ? p : null;
+  } catch {
+    return null;
+  }
+}
+
 function routerEntry() {
+  const declared = declaredRouterEntry();
+  if (declared) return declared;
   for (const ext of [".tsx", ".ts", ".jsx", ".js"]) {
     const p = resolve(APP, "src/router" + ext);
     if (existsSync(p)) return p;
